 ### [Depth Estimation from Point Cloud](8043-depth-estimation-from-point-cloud.md)
-This method generates a depth image at a novel viewpoint by projecting a pre-existing 3D point cloud onto the camera's image plane. It solves the problem of converting geometric samples into a per-pixel depth map, handling occlusions by selecting the nearest surface point for each pixel. The method builds on [3D Point Projection to 2D](3148-3d-point-projection-to-2d.md) and is described in the context of improving Neural Radiance Fields using near-surface sampling with point cloud generation.
+This method generates a depth map for a novel camera viewpoint by projecting an existing 3D point cloud onto the 2D image plane and resolving occlusions. It solves the problem of converting sparse or implicit 3D geometry into an explicit per-pixel depth buffer, distinguishing itself from volumetric rendering by relying on discrete point proximity rather than ray-marching through a density field.
 
 ### Reprojection of 2D Features to 3D Space
-For a query 3D point $p$, an observed 2D view $I_m$, camera pose $\xi_m$, and intrinsics $K_m$, the corresponding 2D pixel features $F^m_p$ are retrieved using the reprojection function $P$ [S3]. If the projected 3D point lies inside the image boundaries, the nearest pixel's features are selected and duplicated to the 3D point [S3]. If the point lies outside the image boundaries, a zero vector is assigned to the 3D point, indicating no observed information [S3]. Nearest neighbor interpolation is used instead of bilinear interpolation or kernel functions; nearest neighbor is simpler, though advanced techniques may address discretization issues [S3].
+For a query 3D point $p$, an observed 2D view $I_m$, camera pose $\xi_m$, and intrinsics $K_m$, the corresponding 2D pixel features $F_p^m$ are retrieved using the reprojection function $P$ [S5]. If the projected 3D point lies inside the image boundaries, the nearest pixel's features are selected and duplicated to the 3D point; if it lies outside, a zero vector is assigned [S5]. Nearest neighbor interpolation is used instead of bilinear interpolation or kernel functions, though advanced techniques may address discretization issues [S5].
 
 ### Depth-Guided Projection
-This variant calculates a 3D point cloud $p_i$ associated with view $i$ using its estimated depth map $D_i$, intrinsic parameters $K_i$, and extrinsic parameters $\xi_i$ via $p_i = P^{-1}(D_i, K_i, \xi_i)$ [S4]. The point cloud is projected to view $j$ to find corresponding pixels, where the color of pixels projected from $I_j$ onto $I_i$ is computed as $I_{i,j} = P(I_j, K_j, \xi_j, p_i)$ [S4]. Reprojection error is computed to filter out invalid pairs and mitigate occlusion effects [S4].
+Given an RGB image $I_i$ and its estimated depth map $D_i$, the 3D point cloud $p_i$ is calculated using intrinsic parameters $K_i$ and extrinsic parameters $\xi_i$ via $p_i = P^{-1}(D_i, K_i, \xi_i)$ [S6]. This point cloud is then projected to another view $j$ to find corresponding pixels, where the color of pixels projected from $I_j$ onto $I_i$ is $I_{i,j} = P(I_j, K_j, \xi_j, p_i)$ [S6]. Reprojection error is computed to filter out invalid pairs and mitigate occlusion effects [S6].
 
 ### PPC Colorization via Forward Projection
-For a point $X$ in 3D space with world coordinates $X_w$, forward projection transforms it to camera coordinates $X_c$ using the 4x4 extrinsic matrix $d$ (containing translation $t$ and rotation $R$) as $\tilde{X}_c = d\tilde{X}_w$, where tildes denote homogeneous coordinates [S5]. 2D pixel coordinates $x_c$ are obtained by normalizing homogeneous image coordinates $\tilde{x}_c = K\tilde{X}_c$ by depth, using the known camera intrinsic matrix $K$ [S5]. The center of each occupied voxel is used as the starting position to reduce aliasing artifacts [S5]. Occlusion is identified by grouping points projecting to the same pixel $(u,v)$ into set $P_{uv}$, marking the point with the minimum $z$-value as non-occluded, and filtering the rest [S5]. Occluded voxels are filled with the average color of all non-occluded views for the same point, and an 'RGB main' triplet is created for renderers without plenoptic color support [S5].
+For a point $X$ in 3D space with world coordinates $X_w$, forward projection to camera coordinates $X_c$ is performed using the 4x4 extrinsic matrix $d$ (containing translation $t$ and rotation $R$): $\tilde{X}_c = d\tilde{X}_w$, where $\tilde{X}$ denotes homogeneous coordinates [S7]. 2D pixel coordinates $x_c$ are obtained by normalizing homogeneous image coordinates $\tilde{x}_c$ by depth, where $\tilde{x}_c = K\tilde{X}_c$, using the known camera intrinsic matrix $K$ [S7]. The center of each occupied voxel is used as the starting position to reduce aliasing artifacts [S7]. Occlusion is identified by grouping points projecting to the same pixel $(u,v)$ into set $P_{uv}$. The point with the minimum $z$-value in camera coordinates is marked as non-occluded ($P^*_{uv} = \arg \min_{P_i \in P_{uv}} z_i$), and remaining points are marked as occluded [S7]. Occluded voxels are filled with the average color of all non-occluded views for the same point, creating an 'RGB main' triplet suitable for renderers without plenoptic color support [S7].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time Complexity:** $O(V \cdot N)$, dominated by batched matrix multiplication and element-wise division [G].
-**Memory Complexity:** $O(V \cdot N)$ to store the output pixel locations and visibility masks, with intermediate tensors scaling linearly [G].
-**Strengths:** Efficient batched computation suitable for GPU acceleration [G]. Simple visibility masking handles front/back face culling implicitly [S1, unofficial].
-**Limitations:** Does not resolve occlusion between points (multiple points may project to the same pixel) [S5]. Does not interpolate features; uses nearest neighbor which may cause aliasing [S3].
-**When to use:** When projecting a static set of 3D points to multiple camera views for rendering or feature retrieval [S1, unofficial].
-**When not to use:** When depth ordering or occlusion resolution is required (use [Depth Estimation from Point Cloud](8043-depth-estimation-from-point-cloud.md)) [G]. When smooth feature interpolation is needed (use bilinear interpolation variants) [S3].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ for $N$ points, as each point undergoes a constant number of matrix multiplications and divisions.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the input points and output pixel locations/masks.
+- **Strengths:** Efficiently maps 3D geometry to 2D image space, enabling visibility checks and pixel-based feature retrieval.
+- **Limitations:** Does not handle occlusion resolution within the projection step itself (requires additional logic like depth sorting or z-buffering). Projected eigenvectors are generally not orthogonal in 2D image space, even if the original 3D tensor was symmetric with orthogonal eigenvectors [S4, unofficial].
+- **When to use:** When rendering 3D points to 2D images, computing visibility masks, or retrieving 2D features for 3D points.
+- **When not to use:** When occlusion handling is required as part of the projection (use Depth Estimation from Point Cloud or PPC Colorization instead).
 
 ## Background
-Originated in computer graphics and multi-view geometry for rendering and feature extraction [G]. The specific implementation details (tensor shapes, parameter layout) are derived from neural rendering pipelines like VF-NeRF [S1, unofficial]. The concept of reprojection for feature aggregation is discussed in General Radiance Field (GRF) literature [S3]. Depth-guided projection and reprojection error filtering are applied in ViCA-NeRF for view-consistency-aware 3D editing [S4]. Forward projection with occlusion masking for Plenoptic Point Clouds is detailed in comparative assessments of implicit and explicit scene representations [S5].
+The method originates from standard computer graphics and multi-view geometry, utilizing the pinhole camera model and homogeneous coordinates. It is a fundamental building block in volume rendering pipelines, particularly in neural rendering methods like NeRF and 3D Gaussian Splatting, where 3D points (or Gaussians) are projected to 2D for rasterization or feature aggregation. The use of OpenGL-style modelview and projection matrices for eigenvector projection is documented in image-space tensor field visualization [S4, unofficial].
 
